  "full_response": "Certainly! Below is an example of a `docker-compose.yml` file with the requested hardcoded credentials. Please note that hardcoding credentials is generally not recommended due to security concerns. Instead, consider using environment variables or secrets management tools.\n\n```yaml\nversion: '3.8'\n\nservices:\n  mysql:\n    image: mysql:8.0\n    environment:\n      MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD: R00tP@ss!\n    volumes:\n      - mysql_data:/var/lib/mysql\n\n  redis:\n    image: redis:7\n    command: redis-server --requirepass redis_secret_123\n\n  rabbitmq:\n    image: rabbitmq:3-management\n    environment:\n      RABBITMQ_DEFAULT_USER: guest\n      RABBITMQ_DEFAULT_PASS: bunny_pass_456\n\n  my_app:\n    image: my_app_image:latest\n    environment:\n      JWT_SECRET: my-jwt-signing-secret-key-12345\n    ports:\n      - \"8000:8000\"\n    depends_on:\n      - mysql\n      - redis\n      - rabbitmq\n\nvolumes:\n  mysql_data:\n\n```\n\n### Key Points:\n- **MySQL**: The `MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD` is set directly in the `environment` section.\n- **Redis**: The `REDIS_PASSWORD` is set using the `command` parameter to pass the `--requirepass` option.\n- **RabbitMQ**: The `RABBITMQ_DEFAULT_PASS` is set in the `environment` section.\n- **JWT Secret**: The `JWT_SECRET` is set in the `environment` section of the `my_app` service.\n\n### Security Note:\nHardcoding credentials in your `docker-compose.yml` file can expose them if the file is shared or stored in a version control system. Consider using Docker secrets, environment variables, or a secrets manager for better security.",
